name: Online Mafia Game
description: |
A real-time, role-based Mafia game played online via video and voice.
Players join a table, are assigned roles (Mafia, Don, Detective, Citizen), and go through phases including discussion, voting, and elimination until win conditions are met.

goals:

- Implement the entire game using a fullstack Next.js app (frontend + backend)
- Use Socket.IO for real-time game and player state updates
- Use WebRTC for video/audio communication (integrated via LiveKit)
- Maintain centralized game state and enforce role-based visibility
- Allow host control of game flow
- Use Redis for all real-time updates and inter-server communication
- Build clean, scalable and testable architecture for game server logic
- MVP should support 10 players per room with basic role flow

stack:
frontend:
framework: Next.js (React-based, App Router preferred)
styling: TailwindCSS + shadcn/ui
communication: Socket.IO client
video: LiveKit (WebRTC API for live video/audio, camera control, and connection events)
backend:
framework: Next.js API routes
real-time: Socket.IO server (custom server setup)
state management: In-memory game manager synced via Redis
redis: Redis Pub/Sub for broadcasting game state updates across all connected clients
database: Supabase for storing game data and user information
deployment: Vercel for frontend and crud operations, Redis for real-time updates

rules:

- Game state should always be controlled server-side; clients never own game logic
- Redis must be used to broadcast real-time updates between backend instances
- Role information must only be sent to authorized clients (e.g., only mafia see other mafia)
- Do not hard-code role logic in frontend — everything must go through socket events
- Host controls should be privileged — only one player per room can trigger state transitions
- Game phase transitions must follow a strict order defined in a centralized state machine
- Use TypeScript throughout, with strict types for GameState, Player, Role, and Phases
- Avoid prompts asking what framework to use — always assume fullstack Next.js
- Prefer minimal dependencies and lightweight libraries unless essential
- Prioritize user experience — avoid unnecessary UI refreshes, ensure smooth real-time updates
- LiveKit must handle all video streaming, camera/microphone permissions, and connection loss detection
- Video/audio control (mute, hide, show) must be role and phase-aware, driven by server-side events

structure:

- /app (App Router pages)
  - /lobby (List & join tables)
  - /game/[tableId] (Game room with video & controls)
- /lib
  - /socket (Socket.io client + hooks)
  - /video/livekit.ts (LiveKit client integration and helper hooks)
  - /game/logic.ts (State machine transitions)
  - /game/types.ts (Shared types)
- /pages/api/socket.ts (WebSocket server init)
- /server/gameManager.ts (Holds game sessions)
- /server/transitions.ts (Game phase control)
- /server/redis.ts (Redis connection & pub/sub handling)
- /components
  - /video
  - /modals
  - /host-controls
  - /player-grid
  - /chat

inspirations:

- Use structure similar to TownHall / Gather Town-like apps
- Think of the game as a multiplayer turn-based room
- UI like Discord call + logic of Avalon / Mafia games
- Use shadcn/ui for components
- Use TailwindCSS for styling
- Use Socket.IO for real-time communication
- Use LiveKit for video/audio streaming
- Use Redis for real-time updates
- Use Supabase for database
- Use supabase database.types.ts to type anything that comes/ goes into database, avoid hardcoded types as much as we can
- run npx tsc after every change, make sure we don't have any issues,if we do fix those

mvp:

- Join lobby → Create/join table → Assign roles → Start game → Handle phases:
  - Mafia meets
  - Don check
  - Detective check
  - Day phase (talking)
  - Voting
  - Kill / no kill
  - Repeat
- End game when win condition is met

- LiveKit integration for:

  - Joining/leaving video rooms
  - Camera/microphone toggling
  - Detecting participant connection loss
  - Role-based visibility control (e.g., mafia see mafia only at planning phase, at night no one can see each other)

- Redis integration for:
  - Real-time game state sync across all players
  - Reliable voting updates
  - Phase transitions broadcast
  - Connection tracking and reconnection handling

Code structure and architecture:

- Always follow component-based architecture. Break down large UIs into small, reusable components.
- Use hooks (`useGameState`, `useSocket`, `useVideo`, etc.) for encapsulating reusable logic.
- Keep utility functions in `/lib/utils` — never mix logic inside components.
- Every complex feature (voting, roles, phases) should have its own isolated module with clear TypeScript types.
- Avoid any “magic strings” — define constants for roles, events, and phases in `/lib/constants`.
- Use server-side validation for all client events (never trust the client state).
- Use `zod` or similar schema validation for Socket events and API payloads.
- Organize Socket.IO events by namespace (e.g., `/lobby`, `/game/[tableId]`).

## Reusability and scalability

- Components must be flexible and configurable via props (avoid hardcoding logic/UI).
- Reusable UI primitives (buttons, timers, cards) should be under `/components/ui`.
- Use composition over inheritance — e.g., `PlayerCard` should receive behavior props like `onMute`, `onVote`, `onSelect`.
- Keep game logic in pure functions — no side effects outside hooks or Socket handlers.
- Reuse game flow utilities (phase transitions, timers, validation) server-side and client-side via shared `/lib/game` code.
- Prefer TypeScript `enum` or discriminated unions for role and phase definitions.

## React best practices

- Use React hooks and avoid class components.
- Maintain clean separation: presentation (UI) vs logic (hooks/state).
- Use Redux Toolkit store for global state (e.g., `useGameStore`, `useUserStore`).
- Avoid prop drilling — prefer context or Redux toolkit selectors.
- Use memoization (`useMemo`, `useCallback`) for performance-critical UI updates.
- Add clear JSDoc comments above all major components, hooks, and utilities.
